"[2610.20354] Understanding NVIDIA Grace-Blackwell Unified Memory Through Placement-Controlled Microbenchmarks (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20354)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20354\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:Understanding NVIDIA Grace-Blackwell Unified Memory Through Placement-Controlled Microbenchmarks\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Nathan Graddon】, 【6†Aaron Jarmusch】, 【7†Sunita Chandrasekaran】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Understanding NVIDIA Grace-Blackwell Unified Memory Through Placement-Controlled Microbenchmarks, by Nathan Graddon and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:NVIDIA Grace--Blackwell systems make CPU--GPU memory increasingly unified at the programming level, but unified addressability does not make the memory system uniform. A CUDA managed allocation on GB200 may be served from GPU-local HBM, accessed through host/system memory, demand-migrated, prefetched, reused after GPU access, or evicted and reloaded under capacity pressure.\nL17: The problem is that these states share a programming interface while exercising different hardware paths and runtime mechanisms, making a single ``Unified Memory bandwidth'' number misleading.\nL18: > This paper presents a placement-controlled microbenchmark study of CUDA Unified Memory on NVIDIA GB200, using GH200 as a Grace--Hopper baseline and GB10/DGX Spark as a validation platform.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · Oraclizer/statesync-gkr · GitHub (ht